<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Football War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hundred Hours' War, Soccer War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Football War was a brief war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> in July 1969, also called the Hundred Hours' War. Its causes were land disputes and the expulsion of Salvadoran migrants from Honduras, and it coincided with riots at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> qualifying matches between the two countries. About 3,000 people died before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> arranged a ceasefire, and a peace treaty followed only in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/football-war.jpg" alt="Football War" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JuanJose1969 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Honduras_and_El_Salvador_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The nickname suggests that a football match caused the war, but its causes lay deeper, in land reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and in immigration and demographic problems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. El Salvador was overpopulated, with too few jobs, and 5 per cent of its farms occupied 70 per cent of the land. Many Salvadorans migrated to Honduras to work on banana plantations, and by 1969 more than 300,000 lived there. A Honduran land reform law, fully enforced by 1967, took land occupied by Salvadoran immigrants and redistributed it to native-born Hondurans, and thousands of Salvadoran labourers were expelled.</p><p>In June 1969 the two countries met in a two-leg qualifier for the 1970 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a>. Honduras won the first game, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> on 8 June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> the second, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 15 June, each accompanied by harassment and riots. On 26 June El Salvador broke off diplomatic ties, saying that around 12,000 Salvadorans had been forced to flee Honduras.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began on 14 July 1969 at about 6 in the evening, when the Salvadoran air force attacked Honduran airfields, mainly Toncontín International Airport, where the Honduran air force kept half of its aircraft. The Salvadoran army then invaded on two fronts, one towards the Sula Valley and the other along the Pan-American Highway towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>. On 15 July the Honduran air force bombed targets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, among them the Ilopango International Airport and oil facilities.</p><p>On 16 July, in the only major battle of the war, Salvadoran troops surrounded Nueva Ocotepeque, and Honduran forces retreated with civilians. Both fronts then stalled for want of ammunition and because of growing Honduran resistance. The war was the last conflict in which piston-engined fighters fought each other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A ceasefire arranged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> took effect at midnight on 18 July and required Salvadoran troops to withdraw within 96 hours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> delayed until it had guarantees for the Salvadorans still in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, and gave way after the OAS threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. Its troops left on 2 August 1969. Most historians agree that civilians accounted for the majority of the losses. Estimates of Salvadorans displaced run from 60,000 to 130,000, and their return to an overpopulated country was one cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvadoran-civil-war">Salvadoran Civil War</a> about a decade later. Trade between the two countries stopped, and a peace treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 30 October 1980.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Football War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1969 El Salvador invaded Honduras in the <b>Football War</b>, advancing quickly before an OAS ceasefire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1969 Honduras fought the so-called <b>Football War</b> with El Salvador, a conflict of about four days over land and migration that killed perhaps 2,000 to 3,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li></ul></section>
